            {
              "id": "11da250d-ed84-36d6-beb1-f671e3943d45",
              "name": "Use of weak MD5 hash for security. Consider usedforsecurity=False",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, hashlib.md5() creates an MD5 digest, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive uses because collision attacks are practical. If this hash is used for integrity checks, signatures, token generation, password-related logic, or any trust decision, an attacker can exploit the weakness to bypass protections.\n\nFor the sink shown, replace hashlib.md5() with a modern hash such as hashlib.sha256() for general-purpose security hashing. If the code is validating authenticity or preventing tampering with a secret key, use hmac.new(..., hashlib.sha256) instead of a bare hash. Only use MD5 with usedforsecurity=False when the purpose is explicitly non-security-related, such as legacy deduplication or checksums, and that intent is certain.\n\nThis change should be applied consistently anywhere the produced digest is stored, compared, or transmitted. After updating the algorithm, review dependent code and persisted values so digest length, interoperability, and verification logic match the stronger hash output.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or a stronger algorithm for security-relevant hashing in Python, and reserve MD5 only for explicitly non-security uses. When authenticity or tamper resistance depends on a secret, use HMAC with SHA-256 rather than a plain digest. For password storage, do not use fast hashes directly; use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. Review any stored digests, protocol fields, or comparisons affected by the algorithm change so all producers and consumers are updated together.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = payload.encode(\"utf-8\")\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\nhmac (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [Python hmac documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hmac.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d03ed76-770b-3336-bb80-3299283993ee",
              "name": "subprocess called with shell=True can lead to command injection when arguments are tainted.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand injection in Python process execution. This code starts a child process with subprocess.Popen and shell=True, which passes the command through the system shell. If any part of command is influenced by external input, shell metacharacters such as ;, &&, |, $, or backticks can change the intended command and execute arbitrary OS instructions.\n\nIn Python, the safest fix for this sink is to avoid the shell entirely and pass a list of arguments to subprocess.Popen with shell=False, which is the default. Build the argument list explicitly instead of concatenating strings, and validate any user-controlled values against a strict allowlist before including them in the command. This prevents the shell from interpreting attacker-supplied input as additional commands.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass untrusted data into shell commands. Replace string-based command construction with an explicit argument array and keep shell=False. Use absolute executable paths where practical, restrict user input to a known set of permitted values, and reject anything outside that set. If shell usage is unavoidable, redesign the interface to remove user control over the command structure and apply strict validation to each input element before execution. Prefer higher-level Python APIs for file, process, and system operations when available so no shell is needed at all.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nallowed_actions = {\"status\", \"start\", \"stop\"}\n\naction = user_input.strip()\nif action not in allowed_actions:\n    raise ValueError(\"Invalid command argument\")\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    [\"/usr/bin/mycommand\", action],\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True,\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d9e913f-c9ee-38b8-b1f2-e294f9bd4b79",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing authentication and authorization issue at the function level. The view processes a request, selects and invokes a trigger function, renders a template, and returns a response without first verifying that the caller is authenticated and permitted to use this endpoint.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control must be enforced before any sensitive action is taken. In this sink, the request reaches get_trigger(name, trigger), may execute trigger_func(user_input), and builds a response using user-controlled inputs. Without explicit access checks, any caller who can reach the route may invoke application behavior that should be restricted to trusted users or specific roles.\n\nThe fix is to require authentication at the view boundary and perform an authorization check before calling the trigger function or rendering the response. For Django-style views, use login_required and permission_required or a direct permission test against request.user. Deny access by default when the user is anonymous or lacks the required permission.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the route with authentication so anonymous users cannot reach the handler. Enforce authorization at the start of the view and again before any privileged operation such as selecting or executing a trigger function. Use a deny-by-default model and grant access through explicit permissions or roles rather than relying on route obscurity.\n\nKeep authorization checks close to the action they protect. If different triggers have different privilege requirements, map each trigger to a specific permission and verify request.user.has_perm before execution. Return 403 for authenticated users without permission and redirect unauthenticated users to login.\n\nAlso avoid composing templates or response bodies directly from untrusted input. Use the framework template engine with autoescaping, validate allowed template names against a fixed allowlist, and restrict callable targets returned by get_trigger so user-controlled values cannot select unsafe behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.core.exceptions import PermissionDenied\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import render_to_string\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.can_view_trigger', raise_exception=True)\ndef _view(request, name, trigger):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n\n    if trigger_func is not None:\n        if not request.user.has_perm('app.can_execute_trigger'):\n            raise PermissionDenied(\"You do not have permission to execute this action.\")\n        trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    context = {}\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        context[\"user_input\"] = escape(user_input)\n\n    template_name = f\"{name}.html\"\n    content = render_to_string(template_name, context=context, request=request)\n    return HttpResponse(content)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango>=4.2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication and authorization](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24dd7192-87cb-353c-bf23-f2b1468baca8",
              "name": "subprocess called with shell=True can lead to command injection when arguments are tainted.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, passing a constructed command string to os.system(cmd) causes the shell to interpret that string, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can change the command that actually runs.\n\nThis is dangerous because shell metacharacters, command separators, variable expansion, and redirection are processed before execution. An attacker who controls part of the command can execute additional programs, read or modify files, or alter application behavior with the privileges of the Python process.\n\nReplace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run() using a list of arguments and shell=False so Python invokes the target program directly without shell parsing. If only a small set of commands or options is allowed, validate input against an explicit allowlist before executing it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from dynamic input. Prefer subprocess.run(), subprocess.check_call(), or subprocess.check_output() with a sequence of arguments and the default shell=False behavior. Pass each command argument as a separate list element so the operating system does not invoke a shell to reinterpret user-controlled content.\n\nIf user input influences the executable name, flags, or file paths, restrict it with an allowlist of expected values and normalize paths before use. Avoid shell features such as pipes, redirects, wildcards, and command substitution in security-sensitive code. If shell invocation is unavoidable, keep the command static, isolate untrusted data from the shell, and apply strict validation before execution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n\ndef run_safe_command(target_path: str) -> None:\n    subprocess.run([\n        \"/usr/bin/example_tool\",\n        \"--input\",\n        target_path,\n    ], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\nshlex (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "280408b5-0c70-3558-ad51-586dc0bf2c45",
              "name": "Using xml.dom.pulldom.parseString to parse untrusted XML data is known to be vulnerable to XML attacks. Replace xml.dom.pulldom.parseString with its defusedxml equivalent function or make sure defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() is called",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nXML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, using xml.dom.pulldom.parseString on attacker-controlled XML can allow external entity expansion, recursive entity expansion, or other parser abuse that leads to denial of service, unexpected file access, or network requests during parsing.\n\nThe sink shown parses user_input directly with the standard library XML parser. For untrusted data, replace xml.dom.pulldom.parseString with the hardened equivalent from defusedxml so dangerous XML features are blocked by default. If the application must keep standard-library XML APIs, initialize defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() before any XML parsing occurs so the parser behavior is hardened process-wide.\n\nThis fix is appropriate here because the vulnerable operation is the direct call to pulldom.parseString(user_input). The safest remediation is to parse the same input through defusedxml.pulldom.parseString instead of relying on callers to sanitize XML text beforehand.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hardened XML parsers for any XML that can be influenced by users, external systems, uploaded files, or serialized messages. In Python, prefer the matching defusedxml module for the parser you need instead of the standard-library XML parser.\n\nIf replacing imports everywhere is not practical, call defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() once during application startup before any XML libraries are used. Also enforce input size limits, avoid resolving external entities, and reject XML features your application does not need, especially DTD processing. Keep XML parsing isolated from sensitive file paths and internal network resources.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import pulldom\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input: str):\n    return pulldom.parseString(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python Documentation: XML security](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [CWE-776: Improper Restriction of Recursive Entity References in DTDs](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-776",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611",
                  "CWE-776"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2c4feb15-e318-3ee5-a0a9-e096a4a70c84",
              "name": "Starting a process with a shell, possible injection detected, security issue.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python occurs when untrusted data is passed into a shell command. The sink shown, os.system(cmd), invokes the system shell, so any attacker-controlled content in cmd can change the command being executed, append additional commands, or alter arguments in unsafe ways.\n\nIn Python, the safest fix is to avoid the shell entirely and execute a fixed program with an explicit argument list. Replace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run(...) using shell=False, validate or constrain any user-controlled values before using them as arguments, and allow only expected commands or parameter values rather than building a command string dynamically.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass concatenated command strings to os.system, subprocess with shell=True, or similar shell-based execution APIs. Use subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen with a list of arguments and the default shell=False behavior. Keep the executable path fixed, separate each argument into its own list element, and reject unexpected input with allowlists, type checks, and strict normalization. If shell features are truly required, redesign the code to avoid direct user influence over the command text and quote data with Python's escaping utilities only as a last resort, not as the primary defense.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef run_safe(target_name: str) -> None:\n    allowed_name = Path(target_name).name\n    subprocess.run([\"/usr/bin/ls\", \"-l\", allowed_name], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\npathlib\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fed86ac-adca-3d48-8012-53f291ab291e",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is missing function-level access control. The view processes a request, dispatches behavior through `get_trigger(...)`, renders content, and returns a response without enforcing authentication or authorization first.\n\nIn Python web applications, a view like this should verify that the caller is authenticated and allowed to invoke the requested action before any sensitive logic runs. As written, an unauthenticated or unauthorized user may reach `trigger_func(user_input)` and influence which template is returned. That creates a direct path to protected functionality and increases the impact of any downstream issues in the triggered code.\n\nFor this sink, add explicit access control at the view entry point and validate authorization before resolving or invoking the trigger. In Django, the idiomatic fix is to require login with `@login_required` and enforce a permission check such as `@permission_required(...)` or an inline `request.user.has_perm(...)` check that rejects unauthorized requests with `PermissionDenied` or an HTTP 403 response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the view with server-side authentication and authorization checks before reading user-controlled parameters, resolving a handler, or executing business logic. In Django, prefer `login_required` for authentication and `permission_required` or explicit `request.user.has_perm(...)` checks for authorization. Apply these checks consistently to every route that exposes privileged behavior.\n\nDo not rely on hidden links, client-side checks, or request parameters such as `name` and `trigger` to control access. Map users to explicit permissions or roles, maintain an allowlist of permitted actions, and reject any unrecognized or unauthorized action with a 403 response. Where dynamic dispatch is required, resolve only from a fixed server-side allowlist rather than directly from user input.\n\nAlso harden the rest of the sink: render templates through Django's templating engine, escape untrusted content before adding it to HTML, and avoid executing trigger functions that are not explicitly approved for the current user. This reduces the chance that an access-control flaw also exposes code execution paths or cross-site scripting behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.core.exceptions import PermissionDenied\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.view_trigger\", raise_exception=True)\ndef _view(request):\n    name = request.GET.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.GET.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n\n    allowed_triggers = {\n        (\"safe\", \"default\"),\n        (\"report\", \"summary\"),\n    }\n    if (name, trigger) not in allowed_triggers:\n        raise PermissionDenied(\"Not authorized for this action\")\n\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        raise PermissionDenied(\"Invalid or unauthorized trigger\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\").render({}, request)\n\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        template += \"<p>XSS: \" + escape(user_input) + \"</p>\"\n\n    return HttpResponse(template)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Django Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication and authorization](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "CWE-306"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "393820c2-e77a-3b0a-babb-393ca29206ea",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nBroken access control in a Python view can allow unauthenticated or unauthorized users to reach functionality that should be restricted. The shown helper reads a query parameter directly from the incoming request, which suggests the surrounding endpoint may be callable without first enforcing authentication and permission checks.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be applied at the route or view entry point, before processing request data or executing business logic. For a handler that uses request.rel_url.query.get(\"user_input\", \"\"), require an authenticated user, verify the user is allowed to perform the action, and fail closed with an HTTP 401 or 403 response when access is not granted.\n\nThis is especially important because reading user-controlled input is often the first step in a code path that returns sensitive data or triggers privileged behavior. Protect the endpoint itself rather than relying on downstream checks, and keep the helper limited to extracting already-authorized input for permitted requests.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnforce authentication and authorization at the start of every view that exposes protected functionality. In aiohttp, perform the check in the route handler or through middleware before reading parameters, querying data, or invoking privileged operations. Use a deny-by-default approach and return 401 for unauthenticated requests and 403 for authenticated users without the required permission.\n\nDo not rely on hidden URLs, client-side checks, or the presence of a query parameter as a gate. Centralize access-control logic in reusable middleware or helper functions, map permissions to specific actions, and verify ownership or role requirements server-side for every request. Log denied access attempts and add tests that confirm anonymous and low-privilege users cannot reach the protected endpoint.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\n\n\ndef get_current_user(request):\n    user = request.get(\"user\")\n    if user is None:\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n    return user\n\n\ndef require_permission(user, permission):\n    permissions = user.get(\"permissions\", set())\n    if permission not in permissions:\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Insufficient permissions\")\n\n\ndef _get_user_input(request):\n    return request.rel_url.query.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n\n\nasync def protected_view(request):\n    user = get_current_user(request)\n    require_permission(user, \"read:user_input\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    return web.json_response({\"user_input\": user_input})\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "43440521-d319-3495-b4ba-129bc01aea86",
              "name": "Detected the use of eval(). eval() can be dangerous if used to evaluate dynamic content. If this content can be input from outside the program, this may be a code injection vulnerability. Ensure evaluated content is not definable by external sources.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a dynamic code evaluation vulnerability. In Python, eval() executes a string as a Python expression, so passing untrusted or externally influenced content into return eval(code) can allow arbitrary code execution in the current process.\n\nBecause eval() runs with the application's interpreter context, an attacker may be able to access objects, call functions, read data, or chain into operating-system level actions depending on what is in scope. For this sink, the fix is to remove eval() and replace it with a safe parser or an explicit allowlist of supported operations. If the intent is to parse Python literals, use ast.literal_eval() instead of eval().\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not evaluate dynamic strings as Python code. Replace eval() with a safer operation that matches the actual requirement: use ast.literal_eval() for literals, json.loads() for JSON, or explicit dispatch tables and normal control flow for selectable behavior. If only a small set of expressions is allowed, validate against a strict allowlist before processing.\n\nKeep untrusted input separate from execution paths, and avoid exposing builtins, globals, or local scope to dynamic evaluation. Where dynamic behavior is required, design a constrained parser or mapping of permitted commands instead of executing user-controlled text.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef safe_evaluate_literal(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-95: Improper Neutralization of Directives in Dynamically Evaluated Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html)\n- [Python Documentation: eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-95",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "441f2617-b411-308b-b7bd-46d32794341f",
              "name": "subprocess called with shell=True can lead to command injection when arguments are tainted.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, passing a constructed command string to os.system(cmd) causes the shell to interpret that string, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can change the command that actually runs.\n\nThis is dangerous because shell metacharacters, command separators, variable expansion, and redirection are processed before execution. An attacker who controls part of the command can execute additional programs, read or modify files, or alter application behavior with the privileges of the Python process.\n\nReplace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run() using a list of arguments and shell=False so Python invokes the target program directly without shell parsing. If only a small set of commands or options is allowed, validate input against an explicit allowlist before executing it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from dynamic input. Prefer subprocess.run(), subprocess.check_call(), or subprocess.check_output() with a sequence of arguments and the default shell=False behavior. Pass each command argument as a separate list element so the operating system does not invoke a shell to reinterpret user-controlled content.\n\nIf user input influences the executable name, flags, or file paths, restrict it with an allowlist of expected values and normalize paths before use. Avoid shell features such as pipes, redirects, wildcards, and command substitution in security-sensitive code. If shell invocation is unavoidable, keep the command static, isolate untrusted data from the shell, and apply strict validation before execution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n\ndef run_safe_command(target_path: str) -> None:\n    subprocess.run([\n        \"/usr/bin/example_tool\",\n        \"--input\",\n        target_path,\n    ], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\nshlex (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "46eff04f-ea3a-31d9-98c7-b4aab4183b34",
              "name": "Starting a process with a shell, possible injection detected, security issue.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(command) passes a string to the operating system shell, which allows shell metacharacters, command chaining, redirection, and variable expansion to be interpreted instead of treated as plain data.\n\nIf any part of command is influenced by external input, an attacker can execute unintended commands with the privileges of the running process. In Python, the safe fix is to avoid shell invocation entirely and execute a specific program with an argument list by using subprocess.run(..., shell=False), which prevents the shell from parsing user-controlled input.\n\nFor this sink, replace os.system(command) with a subprocess call that supplies the executable and each argument separately. If only a limited set of commands is allowed, enforce an explicit allowlist before execution and reject anything outside that set.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from variables or pass untrusted data into os.system, subprocess with shell=True, or similar shell-based APIs. Invoke a fixed executable directly with a list of arguments so Python bypasses shell parsing. Validate each user-controlled value against a strict allowlist of expected commands, flags, or filenames, and reject unexpected characters or values. Run the process with the least privileges necessary and avoid inheriting sensitive environment settings when launching child processes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef run_command(arg: str) -> int:\n    allowed = {\"status\", \"version\"}\n    if arg not in allowed:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command argument\")\n\n    result = subprocess.run(\n        [\"/usr/local/bin/package-tool\", arg],\n        check=False,\n        shell=False\n    )\n    return result.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "47c44dac-29f9-3615-bc70-7019690c6759",
              "name": "Found 'subprocess' function 'Popen' with 'shell=True'. This is dangerous because this call will spawn the command using a shell process. Doing so propagates current shell settings and variables, which makes it much easier for a malicious actor to execute commands. Use 'shell=False' instead.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection through shell execution. In Python, calling subprocess.Popen with shell=True runs the command through a system shell, which causes shell metacharacters, environment settings, expansion rules, and command chaining to be interpreted instead of treated as literal argument data.\n\nWhen the command value can be influenced by external input, an attacker can inject additional commands or alter execution behavior. In this sink, the safe fix is to pass the program and its arguments as a list and use shell=False so Python executes the target process directly without invoking a shell.\n\nIf dynamic input is required, validate it against a strict allowlist and map user choices to known-safe commands or arguments. Avoid building a single command string, and keep stdout and stderr handling explicit as shown in the replacement code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass untrusted data into shell command strings. Replace shell=True with shell=False and provide arguments as a sequence so the operating system does not invoke a shell parser. Prefer fixed executable paths, map user input to predefined options, and validate dynamic values with a strict allowlist such as known filenames, identifiers, or enumerated arguments. If shell features like pipes, globbing, or redirection seem necessary, implement that behavior in Python instead of delegating to a shell. Also run child processes with the least privileges needed and review inherited environment variables when launching external programs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ncmd = [\"/usr/bin/some_command\", \"--option\", safe_value]\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    cmd,\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True,\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "48147daf-7c72-341e-856d-1d9adb126066",
              "name": "Use of exec detected.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution vulnerability. In Python, calling exec(code) runs the supplied string or code object as Python statements in the current interpreter context. If any part of that value can be influenced by an external source, an attacker can execute arbitrary code, read or modify application data, and take control of the process.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to remove exec and replace it with explicit program logic that only allows known operations. Map allowed commands to real functions and invoke those functions directly instead of interpreting strings as code. If the input represents structured data rather than behavior, parse it with a safe format such as JSON instead of executing it.\n\nPython does not provide a safe sandbox for untrusted code through exec. Restricting globals or locals reduces scope but does not make execution safe against a determined attacker. The correct remediation is to avoid dynamic execution for untrusted input entirely and use an allowlist-based dispatcher or a dedicated parser for the expected format.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass untrusted or user-controlled data to exec. Replace dynamic execution with explicit control flow, such as a dictionary that maps allowed operation names to trusted functions. Validate the requested action against a strict allowlist and validate argument types and ranges before use. When the input is configuration or data, parse it with json, ast.literal_eval only for trusted Python literal formats, or another non-executing parser appropriate to the format.\n\nAvoid relying on filtered strings, regex blocking, or restricted globals and locals as a primary defense for exec. Those measures do not reliably prevent code execution in Python. Keep executable behavior in code, keep input as data, and design interfaces so callers select from predefined capabilities rather than supplying code snippets.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\n\ndef subtract(a, b):\n    return a - b\n\n\nALLOWED_OPERATIONS = {\n    \"add\": add,\n    \"subtract\": subtract,\n}\n\n\ndef run_operation(payload: str):\n    data = json.loads(payload)\n    operation = data[\"operation\"]\n    arguments = data.get(\"args\", [])\n\n    if operation not in ALLOWED_OPERATIONS:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported operation\")\n\n    return ALLOWED_OPERATIONS[operation](*arguments)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: exec()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4991315d-4018-3a1b-8c91-4926c6f27649",
              "name": "Detected direct use of jinja2. If not done properly, this may bypass HTML escaping which opens up the application to cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities. Prefer using the Flask method 'render_template()' and templates with a '.html' extension in order to prevent XSS.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a server-side template rendering cross-site scripting risk. Rendering a Jinja2 template directly with untrusted request data can bypass the safer Flask templating flow and makes it easier to return HTML that includes attacker-controlled content without consistent escaping.\n\nIn Python Flask applications, the safer pattern is to render HTML templates through render_template() and keep user-controlled values as template variables. For the sink shown here, request.args.get(\"user_input\", \"\") is passed into template rendering and then returned to the client. If the template or environment is configured unsafely, or if the value is later marked safe, an attacker can inject script content into the response.\n\nUse Flask's render_template() with a .html template stored under the templates directory, and let Jinja autoescaping handle HTML output. Do not build templates from user input, do not disable autoescape for HTML responses, and only allow explicitly sanitized content to be treated as safe HTML.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRender HTML responses with Flask's render_template() instead of calling a Jinja template object's render() method directly for request-driven output. Store templates as .html files in the application's templates directory so Flask enables the expected autoescaping behavior for HTML. Keep untrusted data as plain template variables, avoid using the safe filter or Markup on request data, and do not concatenate user input into template source. If rich HTML input is a business requirement, sanitize it with a dedicated allowlist-based HTML sanitizer before rendering.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom flask import Flask, render_template, request\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(\"/template-xss/jinja\")\ndef template_xss_jinja():\n    user_input = request.args.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n    return render_template(\"template_xss_jinja.html\", path=\"/template-xss/jinja\", user_input=user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nFlask\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [Flask Templating](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templating/)\n- [Jinja Template Designer Documentation - HTML Escaping](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templates/#html-escaping)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4cd1467d-b799-3d52-a087-5378ededda28",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. In a Django view, any function that returns a response without enforcing authentication or authorization can be invoked by unauthorized users if routing exposes it.\n\nThe shown sink renders and returns an HTML template directly from a request handler, but it does not verify that the caller is logged in or permitted to access the resource. In Python web applications, relying on URL obscurity or template naming is not sufficient protection. The view should explicitly require authentication and, when appropriate, a specific permission before rendering the page.\n\nFor this sink, protect the view with Django authentication and authorization decorators so access is denied by default unless the caller satisfies the required policy. This keeps the check close to the endpoint and prevents accidental exposure if the route is enabled or reused elsewhere.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every view that serves non-public content, and add explicit authorization checks for the specific action or resource being accessed. In Django, use login_required for authenticated-only endpoints and permission_required, user_passes_test, or object-level checks for finer-grained policy enforcement.\n\nApply a deny-by-default approach: assume a view is private unless it is intentionally public. Centralize permission names in your app’s auth model, return 403 for authenticated users lacking permission, and avoid exposing internal pages solely through unprotected function-based views. Review URL mappings to ensure sensitive handlers are consistently wrapped by the same access-control policy.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.view_page', raise_exception=True)\ndef _root(request):\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\")\n    return HttpResponse(template.render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django view decorators](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/http/decorators/)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5d7ffd57-ee5f-303e-aefa-f0ba35aaa3ea",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a function-level access control weakness. The view executes sensitive behavior based on request input, but it does not enforce authentication or authorization before calling the selected trigger function and returning content.\n\nIn Python web applications, every entry-point view should verify who the caller is and whether that user is allowed to perform the requested action. In the shown sink, untrusted request data influences both the trigger lookup and the rendered response. Without access control checks, an anonymous or low-privilege user may be able to invoke server-side functionality by choosing a trigger name, and may also reach behavior intended only for authorized users.\n\nThe fix is to require authentication at the view boundary and then apply explicit authorization checks before dispatching to the trigger. Restrict callable triggers to an allowlist instead of directly trusting request-controlled values. If only certain users may access this endpoint, fail closed with a permission check before any trigger execution or template construction.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the view with Django authentication so unauthenticated users cannot reach it. Add explicit authorization checks tied to a role or permission that matches the action performed by the endpoint. Enforce these checks before reading request-controlled routing parameters, selecting a trigger, or executing any business logic.\n\nDo not allow request data to choose arbitrary internal functions. Map externally supplied values to a small server-side allowlist of permitted operations, and reject anything else. Prefer deny-by-default behavior. If object-specific access is involved, verify ownership or tenant scope in addition to broad role checks.\n\nKeep output handling safe as well. Even after access control is added, escape untrusted content before including it in HTML, and avoid dynamically building template names from user input unless they are validated against a strict allowlist.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\nALLOWED_TRIGGERS = {\n    \"safe\": get_trigger(\"safe_name\", \"safe\"),\n}\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.can_run_trigger\", raise_exception=True)\nasync def _view(request):\n    user_input = await _get_user_input(request)\n\n    name = request.GET.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.GET.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    trigger_func = ALLOWED_TRIGGERS.get(trigger)\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Forbidden\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(\"safe_name.html\")\n\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        template += \"<p>XSS: \" + escape(user_input) + \"</p>\"\n\n    return HttpResponse(template)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Django Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication and authorization](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "644b8010-960a-36da-9ca3-b6c5fda7a36a",
              "name": "Using xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring to parse untrusted XML data is known to be vulnerable to XML attacks. Replace xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring with its defusedxml equivalent function or make sure defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() is called",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, parsing attacker-controlled XML with xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring can expose the application to XML-based attacks such as entity expansion and resource-exhaustion conditions. This can lead to excessive CPU and memory use or other parser abuse when the XML input is not trusted.\n\nThe sink shown, node = ET.fromstring(xml), should be replaced with a hardened parser from defusedxml so hostile XML is rejected before it is processed. For this code path, import from defusedxml.ElementTree and call fromstring there instead of the standard library implementation.\n\nIf changing imports broadly is difficult, ensure XML parsing is centralized and only hardened parsing functions are used for untrusted data. Keep XML input size constrained, reject unexpected document structures early, and avoid mixing trusted and untrusted parser entry points in the same codebase.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse defusedxml for all parsing of untrusted XML in Python instead of xml.etree.ElementTree. Replace direct calls to xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring, parse, or XML with the corresponding defusedxml APIs so dangerous XML constructs are blocked by default. Apply the same rule consistently anywhere request bodies, files, messages, or third-party feeds are parsed.\n\nIn addition to switching parsers, enforce input size limits before parsing, validate expected element names and structure after parsing, and fail closed on parser exceptions. Prefer simpler data formats such as JSON when XML features are not required, and keep XML handling isolated behind a small utility so future code cannot accidentally reintroduce unsafe parser calls.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import ElementTree as ET\n\ndef parse_xml(xml: str):\n    node = ET.fromstring(xml)\n    return node\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python XML security documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [OWASP XML Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65d8f510-285e-3c34-b079-c11a947d763c",
              "name": "subprocess call with shell=True identified, security issue.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in process creation. In Python, using subprocess.Popen with shell=True passes the command through a system shell, so any untrusted input concatenated into that command can be interpreted as additional shell syntax and execute unintended commands.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to avoid the shell entirely and invoke the target program directly with an argument list. Pass each argument as a separate list element, keep shell=False, and validate or allowlist user-controlled values before using them. This prevents shell metacharacters from being evaluated and makes command execution behavior predictable.\n\nIf a shell is absolutely unavoidable, do not build the command with string concatenation. Strictly restrict allowed values to a known safe set and separate external input from command structure as much as possible, but the preferred remediation for subprocess.Popen is direct execution without a shell.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse subprocess APIs with a sequence of arguments instead of a single shell command string. Keep shell=False, use absolute executable paths where practical, and avoid composing commands with string interpolation, concatenation, or format operations. Validate user-supplied values with allowlists or strict parsers based on the expected input shape.\n\nWhere possible, replace shell-driven operations with standard-library functions such as pathlib, shutil, glob, or os APIs so no command interpreter is involved. If the process must receive dynamic data, pass it as explicit arguments and capture output safely. Run the child process with the least privileges required and avoid inheriting unnecessary environment variables or working-directory state.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nALLOWED_ACTIONS = {\"status\", \"start\", \"stop\"}\n\ndef run_command(action: str, target: str) -> subprocess.Popen:\n    if action not in ALLOWED_ACTIONS:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid action\")\n\n    safe_target = Path(target).name\n    if not safe_target:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid target\")\n\n    return subprocess.Popen(\n        [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", action, safe_target],\n        shell=False,\n        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n        stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n        text=True,\n    )\n\nprocess = run_command(\"status\", \"service1\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nimport pathlib\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6b1c0a7b-b6c8-340f-b686-064aa32fd8dd",
              "name": "Detected SHA1 hash algorithm which is considered insecure. SHA1 is not collision resistant and is therefore not suitable as a cryptographic signature. Use SHA256 or SHA3 instead.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis code uses an insecure cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, creating a digest with hashlib.sha1() relies on SHA-1, which is no longer considered safe for security-sensitive uses because practical collision attacks exist.\n\nIf this hash is used for signatures, integrity checks, token generation, or any trust decision, an attacker may be able to craft different inputs that produce the same digest. For the sink shown here, replace hashlib.sha1() with a stronger algorithm such as hashlib.sha256() or hashlib.sha3_256(), depending on compatibility requirements. If the value is used for password storage, use a dedicated password hashing function instead of a general-purpose hash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace SHA-1 with a modern collision-resistant hash such as SHA-256 or SHA-3 for all security-relevant hashing. Keep the algorithm consistent across producers and consumers of the digest so compatibility is preserved during migration. If the digest is keyed, use hmac with SHA-256 rather than building a custom construction. If the code is handling passwords, do not use hashlib directly; use a password hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 via a well-supported Python library. Review stored hashes, signatures, and protocol integrations to plan a safe transition away from SHA-1.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\nh = hashlib.sha256()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [NIST Secure Hash Standard (SHS)](https://www.nist.gov/publications/secure-hash-standard-shs)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6bf0ce6a-0162-3ec5-a8c9-72dd8933968c",
              "name": "Avoid using `pickle`, which is known to lead to code execution vulnerabilities. When unpickling, the serialized data could be manipulated to run arbitrary code. Instead, consider serializing the relevant data as JSON or a similar text-based serialization format.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure deserialization of untrusted data. In Python, `pickle.loads()` reconstructs Python objects and can execute attacker-controlled code during deserialization, so it must not be used on data that comes from a user or any untrusted source.\n\nIn this sink, `user_input` is encoded and passed directly to `pickle.loads()`. That means an attacker can supply a crafted payload that triggers arbitrary code execution when the application processes it. For data exchanged with users, replace pickle-based deserialization with a safe text format such as JSON and validate the decoded structure before using it.\n\nIf only simple values are expected, parse the input with `json.loads()` and enforce the expected type and required fields. If the input format cannot be changed immediately, treat all pickled input as unsafe and redesign the interface so only trusted, internally generated data is deserialized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize untrusted input with `pickle`, `cPickle`, `dill`, or similar Python object deserializers. Use `json` for user-controlled data, then validate the parsed value against the exact structure your code expects before accessing fields. Restrict accepted types to primitives and containers such as dictionaries, lists, strings, numbers, and booleans.\n\nWhere structured validation is needed, define a schema or model and reject unexpected keys, missing required fields, and incorrect types. Keep serialization formats language-neutral for external interfaces, and reserve pickle only for trusted, internal data where both producer and consumer are under your control.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef parse_user_input(user_input: str):\n    data = json.loads(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    name = data.get(\"name\")\n    age = data.get(\"age\")\n\n    if not isinstance(name, str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid 'name'\")\n    if not isinstance(age, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid 'age'\")\n\n    return {\"name\": name, \"age\": age}\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "73232df1-c569-3395-9f13-605fb2f880b0",
              "name": "Avoid using `pickle`, which is known to lead to code execution vulnerabilities. When unpickling, the serialized data could be manipulated to run arbitrary code. Instead, consider serializing the relevant data as JSON or a similar text-based serialization format.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is an unsafe deserialization issue. In Python, `pickle.load()` reconstructs arbitrary Python objects, and unpickling attacker-controlled data can execute code during deserialization.\n\nThe sink `return pickle.load(user_input)` is dangerous because it reads serialized content directly from a potentially untrusted source. If an attacker can influence `user_input`, they can supply a crafted pickle payload that triggers arbitrary code execution when loaded.\n\nReplace pickle-based deserialization with a safe data format such as JSON for untrusted input. Parse the incoming data as JSON and validate that the result contains only the expected primitive types and fields before returning or using it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use `pickle`, `cPickle`, or other Python object deserializers on untrusted input. Prefer JSON for externally supplied data because it only represents basic data types and does not instantiate arbitrary Python objects.\n\nValidate the parsed structure before use: require the expected top-level type, restrict allowed fields, and enforce type checks on each value. If you must process serialized data from a trusted internal source, protect its integrity with authentication and ensure it is never attacker-controlled.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input):\n    if hasattr(user_input, \"read\"):\n        data = json.load(user_input)\n    else:\n        if isinstance(user_input, bytes):\n            user_input = user_input.decode(\"utf-8\")\n        data = json.loads(user_input)\n\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    if \"id\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"id\"], int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'id' must be an integer\")\n    if \"name\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"name\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if \"email\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"email\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'email' must be a string\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [Python pickle documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python json documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7a57629a-aea7-36d1-b1b5-a8cdfc47f885",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMissing function-level access control. This view is callable without any authentication or authorization check, so any client that can reach the route may execute application behavior that should be restricted.\n\nIn this Python aiohttp handler, the request is processed directly, a trigger function is resolved and invoked, and a template is returned before any user identity or role is verified. That is especially risky because the route can dispatch to trigger-specific behavior based on request-controlled values. Access control must be enforced at the start of the handler and should verify both that the caller is authenticated and that the caller is allowed to execute the requested action.\n\nThe fix is to require an authenticated user from the request context and deny access unless the user has an allowed role or permission. After authorization succeeds, continue processing the request. If different trigger values represent different capabilities, validate them against an allowlist and check permission for each one explicitly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect every route that performs non-public work with explicit authentication and authorization checks. In aiohttp, enforce this either in middleware or in a shared helper invoked at the top of each handler so unauthenticated requests fail before any business logic runs.\n\nAuthorize based on server-side identity and permissions, not on request parameters. Map each route and sensitive action to a permission, and if the handler can invoke different trigger functions, validate the trigger against a strict allowlist and check permission for that specific action before calling it. Return 401 for unauthenticated users and 403 for authenticated users without access. Keep privileged routes separate from public views to reduce accidental exposure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\n\nALLOWED_TRIGGERS = {\"safe\"}\n\n\ndef require_user(request):\n    user = request.get(\"user\")\n    if user is None:\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n    return user\n\n\ndef require_permission(user, permission):\n    permissions = set(user.get(\"permissions\", []))\n    if permission not in permissions:\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Forbidden\")\n\n\nasync def _view(request):\n    user = require_user(request)\n    require_permission(user, \"view:package_class\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    trigger = request.match_info.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n    name = request.match_info.get(\"name\", \"\")\n\n    if trigger in ALLOWED_TRIGGERS:\n        require_permission(user, f\"trigger:{trigger}\")\n        trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n        if trigger_func:\n            trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\")\n    return web.Response(text=template, content_type=\"text/html\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f18b2d5-9d33-36bc-97ae-4aec5ba666e8",
              "name": "Deserialization of untrusted data with pickle/marshal/shelve can lead to remote code execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization of untrusted data. In Python, pickle.loads() reconstructs arbitrary Python objects, and loading data from a user-controlled source allows attackers to trigger code execution during deserialization.\n\nIn this sink, user_input is converted to bytes and passed directly to pickle.loads(). That means any attacker who can influence the input can supply a crafted pickle payload that executes code on the server before the application has a chance to validate the resulting object. For externally supplied data, use a data-only format such as JSON and validate the parsed structure before using it.\n\nFor this specific case, replace pickle.loads(user_input.encode(\"utf-8\")) with json.loads(user_input), then enforce the expected type and required fields. If the input should be a dictionary or list, reject anything else and fail closed instead of attempting to deserialize arbitrary Python objects.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use pickle, marshal, or shelve to deserialize data that originates from users, HTTP requests, cookies, message queues, or any other untrusted boundary. Prefer JSON for simple data exchange, and validate the decoded value against the exact schema your code expects, including type checks, required keys, allowed values, and size limits.\n\nIf object serialization is required for trusted internal use, restrict it to authenticated and integrity-protected data flows that never accept attacker-controlled content. Keep deserialization code away from external inputs, reject unexpected types, and use explicit parsing into primitive data structures or application-defined models rather than reconstructing executable Python objects.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input: str):\n    data = json.loads(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected JSON object\")\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of untrusted data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "842c028d-e28c-32c8-8f05-f3d0ce144a82",
              "name": "Use of eval/exec/compile on possibly tainted input enables arbitrary code execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution vulnerability. In Python, calling exec() on data that can be influenced by a user allows that data to be interpreted as Python statements and executed with the privileges of the running process.\n\nBecause exec(code) executes arbitrary source text, an attacker can run commands, access files, modify program state, or pivot to other systems. The correct mitigation is to stop treating input as code: replace exec() with explicit application logic, a fixed dispatch table of allowed operations, or safe parsing of structured data formats such as JSON. If the intent is to parse Python literals only, use ast.literal_eval instead of executing statements.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRemove exec() for any path that processes external or variable input. Model the input as data, validate it against a strict allowlist, and map accepted values to predefined functions or methods. For configuration or message payloads, use structured formats such as JSON and validate required fields before acting on them.\n\nIf you only need to parse simple literal values, use ast.literal_eval for trusted literal syntax instead of executing statements. Do not rely on filtering keywords or characters to make exec() safe. When legacy behavior cannot be removed immediately, isolate the functionality behind strong authentication, minimize privileges, and refactor toward explicit non-executable control flow as the permanent fix.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\nALLOWED_ACTIONS = {\n    \"start\": lambda: Package.Class.start(),\n    \"stop\": lambda: Package.Class.stop(),\n    \"status\": lambda: Package.Class.status(),\n}\n\n\ndef run_action(request_body: str):\n    payload = json.loads(request_body)\n    action = payload.get(\"action\")\n\n    if action not in ALLOWED_ACTIONS:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported action\")\n\n    return ALLOWED_ACTIONS[action]()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\nast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: exec()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "879ada17-1f55-367f-9351-21fdb24acb7e",
              "name": "Untrusted user input in `importlib.import_module()` function allows an attacker to load arbitrary code. Avoid dynamic values in `importlib.import_module()` or use a whitelist to prevent running untrusted code.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nExternally controlled module loading. In Python, passing untrusted data into importlib.import_module() allows an attacker to influence which module is imported and executed, which can lead to arbitrary code execution or unintended access to application functionality.\n\nThe sink shown, return import_module(module_name), is dangerous when module_name can be influenced by a request, configuration entry, header, environment variable, or any other untrusted source. Python imports execute module-level code during loading, so a malicious or unexpected module name can trigger code paths the application never intended to expose.\n\nRemediate this by removing dynamic imports where possible. If dynamic selection is required, map fixed user-facing identifiers to a small allowlist of known-safe modules and reject anything else before calling import_module(). This keeps control of the import target inside the application rather than with the caller.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass raw user input, request parameters, headers, cookies, environment variables, or database values directly into importlib.import_module(). Prefer explicit imports when the set of modules is known at development time. When runtime selection is necessary, use a constant allowlist that maps simple external identifiers to fully qualified internal module names, and fail closed for unknown values. Keep allowed modules in trusted code, avoid importing from user-controlled paths, and review imported modules for side effects at import time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n\n_ALLOWED_MODULES = {\n    \"json_parser\": \"Package.safe_json_parser\",\n    \"xml_parser\": \"Package.safe_xml_parser\",\n}\n\n\ndef load_module(module_name: str):\n    try:\n        safe_module = _ALLOWED_MODULES[module_name]\n    except KeyError:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported module selection\")\n\n    return import_module(safe_module)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-470: Use of Externally-Controlled Input to Select Classes or Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html)\n- [Python Documentation: importlib.import_module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP: Unsafe Use of Reflection](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Unsafe_use_of_Reflection)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-470",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-470"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8f69c26e-7711-3f75-90bf-2954da075ed7",
              "name": "Using lxml.etree.parse to parse untrusted XML data is known to be vulnerable to XML attacks. Replace lxml.etree.parse with its defusedxml equivalent function.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nVulnerability label: insecure XML parsing of untrusted input.\n\nParsing attacker-controlled XML with lxml.etree.parse can allow XML-based attacks such as external entity expansion, file disclosure, server-side request forgery, and denial of service from malicious payloads. In Python, this is especially risky when XML is read directly from request data or other untrusted sources without parser hardening.\n\nFor the sink shown, replace direct use of lxml.etree.parse on untrusted data with a hardened parser from defusedxml. This blocks dangerous XML constructs before they are processed and is the recommended mitigation when XML input cannot be avoided.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with the default lxml parsing entry points. Use defusedxml wrappers for parse and fromstring when handling XML from users, requests, files, queues, or third-party systems. If lxml must be retained for other features, ensure untrusted XML is isolated behind hardened parsing and reject documents that rely on DTDs, external entities, or excessive expansion. Also apply input size limits and fail closed on parser exceptions so malformed or hostile XML is not processed further.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom defusedxml.lxml import parse\n\nxml_stream = StringIO(xml)\ntree = parse(xml_stream)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml>=0.7.1\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [lxml Parsing and External Entities Documentation](https://lxml.de/parsing.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "971fb3d3-2e80-39f1-a029-be7c5b5ed5ef",
              "name": "Detected a possible YAML deserialization vulnerability. `yaml.unsafe_load`, `yaml.Loader`, `yaml.CLoader`, and `yaml.UnsafeLoader` are all known to be unsafe methods of deserializing YAML. An attacker with control over the YAML input could create special YAML input that allows the attacker to run arbitrary Python code. This would allow the attacker to steal files, download and install malware, or otherwise take over the machine. Use `yaml.safe_load` or `yaml.SafeLoader` instead.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization. In Python, calling yaml.load with yaml.UnsafeLoader allows specially crafted YAML input to construct arbitrary Python objects during parsing. If user-controlled data reaches this sink, an attacker can trigger code execution, read local files, or alter application behavior.\n\nThe shown sink returns yaml.load(user_input, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader), which is explicitly unsafe for untrusted input. Replace it with yaml.safe_load or yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader) so only standard YAML types are parsed. If the application requires specific object types, do not deserialize them directly from untrusted YAML; map validated primitive fields into trusted Python objects after parsing.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTreat all YAML input as untrusted unless it is generated and protected entirely within your own application boundary. Use yaml.safe_load for parsing and restrict accepted document structure to primitive types such as dict, list, string, number, and boolean. Validate the parsed result before use, including expected keys, value types, and size limits.\n\nDo not use yaml.load with yaml.Loader, yaml.CLoader, yaml.UnsafeLoader, or equivalent unsafe constructors on attacker-controlled data. If you must support richer application objects, parse with SafeLoader first and then explicitly construct trusted Python objects from validated fields rather than allowing the YAML parser to instantiate them automatically.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\ndef parse_user_yaml(user_input: str):\n    data = yaml.safe_load(user_input)\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML>=6.0.1\nimport yaml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [Unsafe YAML deserialization in PyYAML](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9eb7c8b7-70e3-397f-91b1-7cc667f067f8",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nBroken function-level access control. This view function can be reached without any authentication or authorization check before it redirects the caller, which means any user who can hit the route may invoke behavior that should be restricted.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be enforced at the view boundary, not assumed elsewhere in the request flow. For a Pyramid-style view like this one, require an authenticated user and verify the caller has an appropriate permission before returning the redirect. That ensures unauthorized requests are rejected consistently even if the route is discovered directly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the view with framework-native authorization so access is checked before executing the handler. In Pyramid, configure an authentication policy and ACL or security policy, then declare a permission on the view with view_config or an equivalent route binding. Do not rely on the redirect target alone to provide protection, because the function itself is still callable.\n\nApply the same pattern to every sensitive endpoint: require authentication, check the least-privileged permission needed for the action, and return a deny response for unauthorized users. Centralize security configuration where possible, but keep explicit permission requirements on views so the protection remains visible and enforced at the entry point.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom pyramid.httpexceptions import HTTPFound\nfrom pyramid.security import authenticated_userid\nfrom pyramid.view import view_config\n\n@view_config(route_name=\"index\", permission=\"view\")\ndef index(request):\n    if authenticated_userid(request) is None:\n        raise HTTPForbidden()\n    return HTTPFound(location=\"/vulnpy\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npyramid\nfrom pyramid.httpexceptions import HTTPFound, HTTPForbidden\nfrom pyramid.security import authenticated_userid\nfrom pyramid.view import view_config\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)\n- [Pyramid Security](https://docs.pylonsproject.org/projects/pyramid/en/latest/narr/security.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a6c0f725-7a7c-384a-8971-38a555276301",
              "name": "By default, jinja2 sets autoescape to False. Consider using autoescape=True or use the select_autoescape function to mitigate XSS vulnerabilities.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nImproper output encoding in server-side templates can lead to cross-site scripting. In Python, creating a Jinja2 Environment without enabling autoescaping allows HTML-special characters from untrusted data to be rendered directly into the response.\n\nWith the shown sink, Environment(loader=loader), Jinja2 uses its default autoescape behavior, which is unsafe for HTML rendering. If template variables contain attacker-controlled content, the browser may interpret that content as markup or script instead of text. Configure the environment to autoescape HTML-capable templates so values are escaped by default during rendering.\n\nFor Jinja2, the recommended fix is to enable autoescape explicitly, preferably with select_autoescape so escaping is applied to .html, .htm, .xml, and similar template types. This keeps normal text rendering intact while protecting web responses from injected scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnable Jinja2 autoescaping for any templates that generate HTML, XHTML, or XML. Prefer select_autoescape over a blanket boolean so escaping is automatically applied based on template extension and to string-based templates when appropriate. Keep template data separate from markup, and avoid marking content as safe unless it has been sanitized for the exact output context.\n\nAlso review templates for uses of the safe filter, Markup, or direct insertion into JavaScript, CSS, URL, or HTML attribute contexts, because those cases may require context-specific encoding or sanitization beyond standard HTML escaping. Treat all request data, headers, query parameters, form values, and stored content as untrusted until encoded for the destination context.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, select_autoescape\n\nenvironment = Environment(\n    loader=loader,\n    autoescape=select_autoescape(\n        enabled_extensions=(\"html\", \"htm\", \"xml\"),\n        default_for_string=True,\n        default=False,\n    ),\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Jinja2 Environment API](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.Environment)\n- [Jinja2 select_autoescape API](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.select_autoescape)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab752e7e-8dec-3f8d-ad3f-2342ea5048b4",
              "name": "Use of possibly insecure function - consider using safer ast.literal_eval.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code evaluation is present here. In Python, calling eval on data such as return eval(code) treats the input as executable Python source, which allows an attacker to run arbitrary expressions if they can influence that value.\n\nThis is dangerous because eval executes more than simple data parsing. It can access objects, call functions, and trigger unintended behavior depending on the available scope. For input that is expected to be a literal value such as a string, number, list, dict, tuple, boolean, or None, replace eval with ast.literal_eval so only Python literal structures are accepted.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to parse the value as a literal instead of executing it as code. If the application actually needs a structured interchange format, prefer json.loads for JSON input rather than evaluating Python syntax.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use eval on untrusted or variable input. Treat incoming data as data, not executable code. When the expected content is a Python literal, use ast.literal_eval. When the expected content is JSON, use json.loads. If only a small set of values is allowed, validate against an allowlist before parsing. Keep parsing logic separate from execution logic so user input can never reach dynamic code execution sinks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef parse_code(code):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Direct Dynamic Code Evaluation - Eval Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Direct_Dynamic_Code_Evaluation_Eval%20Injection)\n- [CWE-95: Improper Neutralization of Directives in Dynamically Evaluated Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html)\n- [Python documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n- [Python documentation: eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-95",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c2381e64-4652-37de-847a-7576837b5c81",
              "name": "Use of eval/exec/compile on possibly tainted input enables arbitrary code execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution via untrusted input. In Python, passing attacker-controlled data to compile() turns text into executable code objects. If that code object is later executed, an attacker can run arbitrary Python statements with the privileges of the application.\n\nThe sink shown compiles the variable code in exec mode, which is intended for full statement blocks rather than simple expressions. That makes the impact especially severe because the input can contain imports, function definitions, file access, subprocess calls, or other harmful operations. The fix is to stop compiling raw input as Python code and instead parse and validate only the limited data or expression format the application actually needs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use compile(), eval(), or exec() on untrusted input. Treat external input as data, not Python source code. If the feature only needs structured input, use json.loads(), pydantic models, or explicit parsing instead of dynamic execution. If expression support is required, restrict input to a small grammar, parse it with ast.parse(..., mode=\"eval\"), reject unsafe node types such as Call, Attribute, Name, Import, Lambda, comprehensions, and assignment constructs, and only then compile the validated AST in eval mode. Prefer an allowlist of permitted syntax and operations over trying to block dangerous keywords.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\n\ndef parse_user_expression(code: str):\n    tree = ast.parse(code, mode=\"eval\")\n\n    allowed_nodes = (\n        ast.Expression,\n        ast.BinOp,\n        ast.UnaryOp,\n        ast.Constant,\n        ast.Add,\n        ast.Sub,\n        ast.Mult,\n        ast.Div,\n        ast.Mod,\n        ast.Pow,\n        ast.USub,\n        ast.UAdd,\n        ast.Load,\n    )\n\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if not isinstance(node, allowed_nodes):\n            raise ValueError(\"Unsupported expression\")\n\n    return compile(tree, \"vulnpy.py\", \"eval\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nast (Python standard library)\njson (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: compile()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#compile)\n- [Python Documentation: ast module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c5b49cf2-f7b0-376e-a2ff-97265467e8c3",
              "name": "Use of weak SHA1 hash for security. Consider usedforsecurity=False",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, calling hashlib.sha1() selects SHA-1, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive uses because collision attacks against SHA-1 are practical. If this digest is used for signatures, integrity checks, token derivation, password-related logic, or other trust decisions, an attacker may be able to exploit those weaknesses.\n\nReplace SHA-1 with a stronger hash such as SHA-256 or SHA-512 for security purposes. If the code is only computing a non-security checksum for deduplication or compatibility, make that intent explicit by passing usedforsecurity=False where supported, so the code does not rely on SHA-1 as a security control.\n\nFor the sink shown here, the direct fix is to change hashlib.sha1() to hashlib.sha256() when the result protects data or influences authorization or integrity decisions. Reserve SHA-1 with usedforsecurity=False only for legacy interoperability or non-adversarial fingerprinting where collisions do not create a security impact.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or stronger for any security-relevant hashing. In Python, prefer hashlib.sha256(), hashlib.sha384(), or hashlib.sha512() for general-purpose cryptographic hashing. If you need message authentication, use hmac with a strong hash rather than a raw digest. For passwords, use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 instead of hashlib.\n\nOnly keep SHA-1 for legacy compatibility or non-security fingerprinting, and in that case call hashlib.sha1(usedforsecurity=False) when the runtime supports it. Review all consumers of the digest to ensure the value is not used for authentication, integrity enforcement, signature generation, or any other security decision.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = b\"example payload\"\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Secure Hash Standard (FIPS 180-4)](https://www.nist.gov/publications/secure-hash-standard-shs-fips-180-4)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cb17dcd5-5c8d-3474-b836-fdc0e7c4a020",
              "name": "Starting a process with a shell, possible injection detected, security issue.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection through shell process execution. In Python, calling os.system(cmd) passes the command string to a shell, which interprets metacharacters, command separators, variable expansion, and substitutions. If any part of cmd is influenced by external input, an attacker can execute unintended commands.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to stop invoking a shell and run the target program directly with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False. This prevents shell parsing and treats each argument as data. If user input must affect behavior, validate it against a strict allowlist before passing it as an argument.\n\nAlso avoid building commands by string concatenation or formatting. Prefer fixed executable paths, explicit argument arrays, and input validation for expected values such as filenames, flags, or identifiers. Raise errors on invalid input instead of attempting to sanitize arbitrary shell syntax.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run([...], shell=False, check=True) and pass arguments as a sequence, not a single command string. Keep the executable and flags fixed whenever possible, and validate any variable input with strict allowlists based on the expected format. For paths, resolve them and enforce that they stay within an approved directory.\n\nDo not rely on shell escaping as the primary defense, and do not use shell=True unless there is a documented, unavoidable requirement. If a shell is absolutely necessary, minimize input influence, use fixed command templates, and validate each allowed value narrowly. Prefer Python standard-library APIs for file, process, and system tasks instead of invoking shell utilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n_SAFE_NAME = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$\")\n_BASE_DIR = Path(\"/srv/app/data\").resolve()\n\n\ndef run_safe_command(user_value: str) -> None:\n    if not _SAFE_NAME.fullmatch(user_value):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid argument\")\n\n    target = (_BASE_DIR / user_value).resolve()\n    if _BASE_DIR not in target.parents and target != _BASE_DIR:\n        raise ValueError(\"path traversal detected\")\n\n    subprocess.run([\n        \"/bin/ls\",\n        \"-l\",\n        str(target),\n    ], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\npathlib\nre\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Prevent Command Injection in Python subprocess calls](https://securecodingpractices.com/prevent-command-injection-python-subprocess/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cedba811-ad8d-3d44-b846-d20a242f5e32",
              "name": "Use of eval/exec/compile on possibly tainted input enables arbitrary code execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution vulnerability. In Python, calling eval on data that can be influenced by a user allows that input to be treated as Python code and executed inside the process.\n\nThe sink shown, return eval(code), is dangerous because any attacker-controlled value in code can run arbitrary expressions, access objects in scope, or trigger system-level actions through imported modules. This can lead to remote code execution, data exposure, or full application compromise.\n\nThe fix is to remove eval and replace it with a safe parser that matches the expected input format. If the value is meant to be a Python literal such as a number, list, dict, boolean, or string, use ast.literal_eval. If only a small set of operations is allowed, map explicit command names to safe functions instead of interpreting raw code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute untrusted input with eval, exec, or compile. Parse the input as data, not as code. For structured values, prefer json.loads or ast.literal_eval depending on the accepted format. For behavior selection, use an allowlist that maps known input values to specific functions. If dynamic evaluation is unavoidable, redesign the interface so the evaluated content is fully controlled by trusted code and not derived from external input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef evaluate_code(code):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n- [Python eval() documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d5153ce7-eb32-3e5a-aaa1-b6a2fa505ca3",
              "name": "Use of weak MD5 hash for security. Consider usedforsecurity=False",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. This code uses MD5 to hash input data in Python, which is not suitable for security-sensitive purposes because MD5 is vulnerable to collision attacks and should not be relied on for integrity checks, signatures, token derivation, or password-related logic.\n\nFor the sink shown, replace hashlib.md5(...) with a modern hash from hashlib such as hashlib.sha256(...) when you need a general-purpose security hash. If the value is being used for password storage, do not use a fast hash at all; use a dedicated password hashing function instead. If the hash is truly non-security metadata such as a legacy checksum, explicitly mark that intent with usedforsecurity=False where supported so the code does not imply a security guarantee.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hashlib.sha256 or a stronger approved algorithm for security-relevant hashing in Python. Keep the input as bytes, and avoid MD5 and SHA-1 for new code. If you need password storage or password verification, use a slow password-hashing algorithm such as Argon2, bcrypt, or PBKDF2 instead of a general-purpose digest.\n\nOnly use md5(..., usedforsecurity=False) for clearly non-security use cases such as compatibility checksums or duplicate detection, and document that intent in code. Review any downstream comparison, token generation, cache key, or integrity logic that depends on this digest so the stronger algorithm is applied consistently across producers and consumers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n\ndigest = hexlify(sha256(user_input).digest()).decode(\"utf8\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d92def60-9506-32ed-baa9-653c374a71a0",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. A Django view path that reads user-supplied input is reachable without enforcing authentication or permission checks, which can allow unauthenticated or unauthorized callers to invoke sensitive application behavior.\n\nIn Python Django code, access control should be applied at the view entry point, not only inside helper functions such as this request-parsing routine. The shown function extracts input from GET parameters, the query string, or POST data, but nothing in the surrounding flow guarantees that the caller is authenticated or has the required permission before that input is used.\n\nProtect the view that calls this helper with Django authentication and authorization controls such as login_required, user_passes_test, or permission_required. If the endpoint is class-based, use LoginRequiredMixin and PermissionRequiredMixin so every request is checked before user-controlled data is processed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication and explicit authorization on every view that exposes protected functionality. Apply decorators or mixins directly to the view that receives the request, and define a concrete Django permission instead of relying on obscurity or client-side checks. If different HTTP methods have different sensitivity, enforce permissions for each method consistently.\n\nKeep helper functions focused on parsing input and move all access decisions to the request entry point. Use least-privilege permissions, deny by default, and return 403 for authenticated users who lack access. Review URL routes for views that call this helper to ensure none are left unprotected, including async views, alternate endpoints, and class-based view methods.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.view_sensitive_resource\", raise_exception=True)\nasync def package_class_view(request):\n    user_input = await _get_user_input(request)\n    return await _handle_user_input(request, user_input)\n\n\nfrom django.contrib.auth.mixins import LoginRequiredMixin, PermissionRequiredMixin\nfrom django.views import View\n\nclass PackageClassView(LoginRequiredMixin, PermissionRequiredMixin, View):\n    permission_required = \"app.view_sensitive_resource\"\n\n    async def get(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n        user_input = await _get_user_input(request)\n        return await _handle_user_input(request, user_input)\n\n    async def post(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n        user_input = await _get_user_input(request)\n        return await _handle_user_input(request, user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django login_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
