            {
              "id": "e4e927eb-c261-3472-8803-e184891e5b9a",
              "name": "Detected a possible YAML deserialization vulnerability. `yaml.unsafe_load`, `yaml.Loader`, `yaml.CLoader`, and `yaml.UnsafeLoader` are all known to be unsafe methods of deserializing YAML. An attacker with control over the YAML input could create special YAML input that allows the attacker to run arbitrary Python code. This would allow the attacker to steal files, download and install malware, or otherwise take over the machine. Use `yaml.safe_load` or `yaml.SafeLoader` instead.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization is present in this Python code. Using PyYAML with `yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)` allows specially crafted YAML documents to instantiate arbitrary Python objects and trigger code execution during parsing.\n\nIn Python, YAML loaders such as `yaml.UnsafeLoader`, `yaml.Loader`, and `yaml.CLoader` should not be used on data that can be influenced by users. The shown sink parses attacker-controlled input with an unsafe loader, which can lead to arbitrary command execution, file access, or full process compromise.\n\nReplace the unsafe loader with `yaml.safe_load_all` or `yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)` so only standard YAML types are constructed. If the application requires only one document, prefer `yaml.safe_load`; if it accepts multiple YAML documents as shown here, use `yaml.safe_load_all` and validate the resulting structure before using it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nOnly deserialize YAML from untrusted sources with `yaml.safe_load` or `yaml.safe_load_all`, or explicitly pass `Loader=yaml.SafeLoader`. Treat all externally supplied YAML as untrusted, and validate the parsed data against the expected schema, keys, and types before using it. If multiple YAML documents are not required, reject them and accept a single document with `yaml.safe_load` to reduce parser complexity. Avoid custom constructors for untrusted input, keep PyYAML updated, and prefer simple data formats when object deserialization is not necessary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\nresult = list(yaml.safe_load_all(user_input))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [PyYAML unsafe load remote code execution overview](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e71b572d-95da-3d5f-889e-9cb4b40de582",
              "name": "Deserialization of untrusted data with pickle/marshal/shelve can lead to remote code execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization. In Python, calling pickle.load on data influenced by a user is dangerous because the pickle format can execute arbitrary code during object reconstruction, which can lead to remote code execution.\n\nThe sink shown, return pickle.load(user_input), deserializes untrusted input directly. If an attacker can control the bytes read by user_input, they can craft a malicious payload that runs code as soon as it is loaded. For data exchanged with clients or external systems, use a non-executable serialization format such as JSON and validate the parsed structure before returning it.\n\nIf legacy pickled data must still be handled, do not accept it from untrusted sources. Restrict deserialization to trusted, authenticated data only, add integrity protection such as a server-generated signature, and migrate the interface toward a safe schema-based format as soon as possible.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace pickle, marshal, and shelve for any data that can originate outside the trust boundary. Prefer JSON for simple structures, then enforce expected types, required fields, length limits, and allowed values before using the result. Keep deserialization code narrow and explicit so only known-safe structures are accepted.\n\nWhen compatibility requires serialized Python objects, treat them as trusted-only artifacts. Store them server side, protect them with authentication and integrity checks, and never load them directly from request data, uploaded files, cookies, or message payloads controlled by users. Review nearby code paths for other unsafe loaders such as pickle.loads, marshal.load, shelve.open, yaml.load with unsafe loaders, or similar object reconstruction APIs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\nfrom typing import BinaryIO, Any\n\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input: BinaryIO) -> Any:\n    raw = user_input.read()\n    if isinstance(raw, bytes):\n        raw = raw.decode(\"utf-8\")\n\n    data = json.loads(raw)\n\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\ntyping\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python pickle module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python json module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
