            {
              "id": "eeba82c4-dcb8-3f2c-92b8-e7b576328a55",
              "name": "Use of weak SHA1 hash for security. Consider usedforsecurity=False",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm use. In Python, creating a digest with hashlib.new(\"SHA1\") selects SHA-1, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive purposes because collision attacks are practical and can undermine integrity checks, signatures, and other security decisions based on the hash.\n\nFor this sink, replace SHA-1 with a stronger hash such as SHA-256 when the digest is used for security. If the code is only performing a non-security compatibility task and must keep SHA-1, make that intent explicit by passing usedforsecurity=False on Python versions that support it. Prefer a modern algorithm by default so the code remains safe across environments and compliance settings.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512 for new security-sensitive hashing in Python. Avoid SHA-1 for integrity validation, token generation, password handling, or signature-related workflows. For passwords, use a dedicated password hashing function such as hashlib.scrypt or a purpose-built password hashing library rather than a general-purpose digest.\n\nIf SHA-1 is required only for legacy interoperability or non-security fingerprinting, document that requirement and construct the hash with usedforsecurity=False where supported, for example hashlib.new(\"sha1\", usedforsecurity=False). Review all call sites to ensure the resulting digest is never reused in an authentication, authorization, or tamper-detection decision.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\nh = hashlib.new(\"sha256\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f0e82ca8-9b70-33ca-9cf6-4f51ed8a955c",
              "name": "Using xml.sax.parseString to parse untrusted XML data is known to be vulnerable to XML attacks. Replace xml.sax.parseString with its defusedxml equivalent function or make sure defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() is called",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is an unsafe XML parsing issue. In Python, parsing untrusted XML with the standard XML SAX parser can expose the application to entity expansion, external entity processing, and denial-of-service conditions caused by malicious XML payloads.\n\nThe sink shown uses sax.parseString(user_input, ContentHandler()) directly on attacker-controlled data. For this code path, use the hardened parser from defusedxml instead of the standard library entry point. That replacement disables dangerous XML features for untrusted input and is the appropriate fix when SAX-style parsing must be preserved.\n\nIf XML input is not strictly required, prefer simpler data formats such as JSON for externally supplied data. When XML must be accepted, combine a hardened parser with input size limits and strict application-level validation of the parsed content.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with xml.sax.parseString from the standard library. Replace it with defusedxml.sax.parseString for any externally influenced input, including request bodies, uploaded files, and message payloads. Keep DTDs and entity expansion disabled for untrusted documents, and reject oversized XML before parsing to reduce denial-of-service risk.\n\nApply allowlist validation to the resulting data model after parsing rather than trusting the XML structure itself. If existing code relies on the standard library XML modules broadly, initialize the hardened protections early with defusedxml.defuse_stdlib(), but prefer direct use of defusedxml APIs at each untrusted parsing call site for clarity and maintainability.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom xml.sax.handler import ContentHandler\nfrom defusedxml import sax\n\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input: str):\n    return sax.parseString(user_input, ContentHandler())\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python xml.sax documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.sax.html)\n- [OWASP XML External Entity Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [CWE-776: Improper Restriction of Recursive Entity References in DTDs](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-776",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611",
                  "CWE-776"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2c2c748-1d70-3571-9c7f-077458523b83",
              "name": "Using lxml.etree.fromstring to parse untrusted XML data is known to be vulnerable to XML attacks. Replace lxml.etree.fromstring with its defusedxml equivalent function.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nXML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, parsing attacker-controlled XML with lxml.etree.fromstring can allow XML-based attacks such as external entity expansion, server-side file access, network access, and denial of service through malicious payloads.\n\nThe sink shown parses user_input directly with etree.fromstring, so the parser may process dangerous XML constructs before the application validates the content. For untrusted XML, use a hardened parser that disables unsafe features. Replace direct parsing with the defusedxml equivalent so hostile documents are rejected during parsing rather than handled by the default XML parser behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with the default lxml parsing entry points. Use defusedxml wrappers for XML parsing so entity expansion and related dangerous features are blocked by default. If XML must be processed with lxml-specific APIs, ensure external entities, DTD loading, and network access are disabled explicitly and reject oversized or deeply nested documents. Treat all XML from requests, files, message queues, and third-party systems as untrusted input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml.lxml import fromstring\n\ndef parse_xml(user_input: str):\n    return fromstring(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [OWASP XML Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [lxml XML Parsing Documentation](https://lxml.de/parsing.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f44ed38d-2b9f-3358-9b44-8416e66ae035",
              "name": "subprocess called with shell=True can lead to command injection when arguments are tainted.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, passing a constructed command string to os.system(cmd) causes the shell to interpret that string, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can change the command that actually runs.\n\nThis is dangerous because shell metacharacters, command separators, variable expansion, and redirection are processed before execution. An attacker who controls part of the command can execute additional programs, read or modify files, or alter application behavior with the privileges of the Python process.\n\nReplace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run() using a list of arguments and shell=False so Python invokes the target program directly without shell parsing. If only a small set of commands or options is allowed, validate input against an explicit allowlist before executing it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from dynamic input. Prefer subprocess.run(), subprocess.check_call(), or subprocess.check_output() with a sequence of arguments and the default shell=False behavior. Pass each command argument as a separate list element so the operating system does not invoke a shell to reinterpret user-controlled content.\n\nIf user input influences the executable name, flags, or file paths, restrict it with an allowlist of expected values and normalize paths before use. Avoid shell features such as pipes, redirects, wildcards, and command substitution in security-sensitive code. If shell invocation is unavoidable, keep the command static, isolate untrusted data from the shell, and apply strict validation before execution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n\ndef run_safe_command(target_path: str) -> None:\n    subprocess.run([\n        \"/usr/bin/example_tool\",\n        \"--input\",\n        target_path,\n    ], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\nshlex (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f87ddd31-3772-367a-b44a-dbebfeecd741",
              "name": "Potential Missing Function Level Access Control. View function lacks authentication/authorization decorators.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a broken access control issue. The Python route handler is exposed without an authentication or authorization check, so any caller who can reach the endpoint can trigger its behavior and follow the redirect to the protected area.\n\nIn this aiohttp-style handler, the route defined at \"/\" immediately redirects to \"/vulnpy\" and does not verify the user's identity, session, or permissions first. Function-level access checks should be enforced before executing the handler logic so unauthenticated or unauthorized requests are rejected consistently.\n\nFor this sink, add an explicit authentication and authorization check at the start of the handler or enforce it centrally with middleware. Return an HTTP 401 response for unauthenticated users and HTTP 403 for authenticated users who lack the required permission, and only perform the redirect after those checks pass.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication and authorization on every non-public route, even when the handler only redirects or returns small amounts of data. In Python web applications, enforce access control as close to the entry point as possible by using middleware, decorators, or shared helper functions so checks are not missed on individual handlers.\n\nUse server-side session validation or trusted identity tokens, and make authorization decisions based on roles or permissions stored by the application, not on client-supplied parameters. Distinguish authentication failures from permission failures with 401 and 403 responses, keep a default-deny posture, and review route registration to ensure newly added endpoints are protected unless explicitly intended to be public.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\nfrom aiohttp_session import get_session\n\nroutes = web.RouteTableDef()\n\n@routes.get(\"/\")\nasync def index(request: web.Request) -> web.StreamResponse:\n    session = await get_session(request)\n    user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\n    roles = session.get(\"roles\", [])\n\n    if not user_id:\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized()\n\n    if \"user\" not in roles:\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden()\n\n    raise web.HTTPFound(\"/vulnpy\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp-session\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Advanced: Middlewares](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_advanced.html#middlewares)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb71f94a-6752-3d13-8ebb-d15edcf086a7",
              "name": "subprocess called with shell=True can lead to command injection when arguments are tainted.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, passing a command string to the operating system with os.system executes it through a shell, so any untrusted data included in that string can change the command being run. An attacker can inject shell metacharacters, append additional commands, or alter arguments.\n\nFor this sink, avoid building a shell command from external input and do not use os.system for dynamic execution. Invoke the target program directly with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False so the input is treated as data instead of shell syntax.\n\nIf only a limited set of commands or arguments is allowed, enforce an allowlist before execution. Validate and normalize user-controlled values, reject unexpected characters or options, and keep the executable name fixed rather than accepting it from input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system(command) with subprocess.run([...], shell=False) and pass arguments as a sequence, not a single shell-interpreted string. Keep the executable path constant, validate each user-controlled argument against strict allowlists or expected formats, and reject values that are not explicitly permitted. If shell usage is unavoidable, redesign the interface to remove user control over the command structure; quoting alone is not a complete defense for attacker-controlled command text. Run the process with the least privileges needed and avoid inheriting sensitive environment variables when executing external programs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef run_command(user_arg: str) -> int:\n    allowed_values = {\"status\", \"start\", \"stop\"}\n    if user_arg not in allowed_values:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command argument\")\n\n    result = subprocess.run(\n        [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", user_arg],\n        check=False,\n        shell=False\n    )\n    return result.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd5efed1-3906-377f-8293-1683f694011e",
              "name": "Starting a process with a shell, possible injection detected, security issue.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python occurs when untrusted data is passed into a shell command. The sink shown, os.system(cmd), invokes the system shell, so any attacker-controlled content in cmd can change the command being executed, append additional commands, or alter arguments in unsafe ways.\n\nIn Python, the safest fix is to avoid the shell entirely and execute a fixed program with an explicit argument list. Replace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run(...) using shell=False, validate or constrain any user-controlled values before using them as arguments, and allow only expected commands or parameter values rather than building a command string dynamically.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass concatenated command strings to os.system, subprocess with shell=True, or similar shell-based execution APIs. Use subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen with a list of arguments and the default shell=False behavior. Keep the executable path fixed, separate each argument into its own list element, and reject unexpected input with allowlists, type checks, and strict normalization. If shell features are truly required, redesign the code to avoid direct user influence over the command text and quote data with Python's escaping utilities only as a last resort, not as the primary defense.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef run_safe(target_name: str) -> None:\n    allowed_name = Path(target_name).name\n    subprocess.run([\"/usr/bin/ls\", \"-l\", allowed_name], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\npathlib\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
